Environment: Linux (opsys.cs.umsl.edu), gcc, VS Code

How to compile the project:
    Type 'make'

Example of how to run the project:
    ./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 4 -i 0.6 -f log.txt

Command-line options:
    -h              Print help and exit
    -n proc         Total number of user processes to launch (default 5)
    -s simul        Maximum simultaneous processes, up to 18 (default 3)
    -t timelimit    Upper bound in seconds for each process's CPU burst (default 4)
    -i interval     Minimum fractional seconds between child launches (default 0.5)
    -f logfile      Output log file name (default log.txt)

Generative AI used: Claude (Anthropic)

Prompts:
    /init
    Based on the requirements lets make a plan on how to implement this project
    Lets execute the plan

Summary: Claude Code was used to generate the full implementation plan and all
source files (shared.h, oss.c, user.c, Makefile) for this project. The generated
code correctly models the round-robin scheduling loop, shared memory clock,
message queue dispatch/reply protocol, blocked queue with 100ms I/O delay,
and SIGINT cleanup. Some minor tuning (overhead values, edge-case clock math)
was reviewed and adjusted after generation. Overall the AI assistance was very
useful for quickly scaffolding the IPC structure and scheduling logic, though
careful review was still needed to ensure correctness of the message protocol
and termination logic.

Implementation Plan:

  Phase 1 - Shared Infrastructure (shared.h + Makefile)
    - struct PCB (as spec'd), struct SharedData (clock + process table),
      struct Message (mtype + value)
    - Constants: BASE_QUANTUM_NS=25000000, MAX_PROCS=20, SHM_KEY, MSG_KEY
    - Makefile with suffix rules, builds both oss and user

  Phase 2 - OSS Skeleton + One Child (IPC proof of concept)
    oss.c:
    - Parse args (-h -n -s -t -i -f)
    - Set up shared memory for SharedData, set up message queue
    - Register SIGINT handler -> cleanup (removes shm + msgq, kills children)
    - Fork one user process, fill its PCB, add to ready queue
    - Send it one quantum via msgsnd, msgrcv the reply, log it
    user.c:
    - Attach to shared memory (read-only clock access)
    - Seed srand with getpid() for unique per-process seeds
    - Receive message from OSS (mtype = own pid), send reply (mtype = 1)

  Phase 3 - Full Scheduling Loop (multiple processes, round-robin)
    oss.c main loop:
    1. Maybe fork a new child (check simul limit, launch interval, wall clock)
    2. Check blocked queue -- unblock any whose eventWaitSec:Nano <= clock
    3. If ready queue non-empty:
         - log ready queue
         - dispatch front (msgsnd), add small scheduling overhead to clock
         - msgrcv reply; interpret: terminated / blocked / full quantum
         - update PCB serviceTime, advance clock by time used, re-queue or remove
    4. Else (no ready processes): advance clock to next launch time
    5. Every 0.5 sim-seconds: print process table to log + screen
    6. Check wall clock -- terminate if > 3 real seconds
    Process launch: track nextLaunchSec/Nano, only fork if clock >= nextLaunch
    and activeCount < s and launched < n; cpuBurst = random 0..t seconds

  Phase 4 - User Process Decision Logic
    Each time user receives a quantum:
    1. If totalServiceTime + quantum >= cpuBurst -> terminate: send -(remaining)
    2. Else roll 20% chance for I/O block:
         Blocked:   pick random 1-99% of quantum, send that value positive
         Full run:  send full quantum value positive
    OSS on reply:
         Negative          -> clear PCB, waitpid, decrement active, advance clock
         Positive < quantum -> blocked: set eventWaitSec/Nano = clock + 100ms
         Positive == quantum -> full run: push back to end of ready queue

  Phase 5 - Statistics & Final Report
    Track in OSS:
    - totalCpuNs:  sum of all values received from processes (actual running time)
    - totalOssNs:  time OSS incremented for its own overhead
    - totalClockNs: final clock value at termination
    Print at end:
    - Simulated clock at exit, total launched/finished
    - Total CPU time, OSS overhead time
    - CPU utilization = totalCpuNs / totalClockNs * 100%

  Key Details:
    Message targeting:  mtype = child_pid (OSS->child); mtype = 1 (child->OSS)
    Clock overflow:     normalize ns >= 1e9 into seconds on every add
    cpuBurst storage:   parallel arrays burstSec[20]/burstNano[20] in OSS
    Zombie prevention:  waitpid(pid, NULL, 0) after termination reply
    Wall-clock timeout: time(NULL) compared to start time each iteration
    Log line cap:       stop writing at 10000 lines, keep simulating
    Process table print: every 500ms simulated time via lastPrintSec tracking

Notes: I am using Claude Code and with this project I created a project specific agent
using the /init command with the project 4 specs pdf already added to the project folder.
This sped things up a lot. The /init took about 3 min to run and then the planning stage
took less then 2 min. The execution of the plan took about 5 mins. So then I just had to 
review the code as it was suggested and test.
